# Cloudflare Pages redirects for the app.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. The old shell handed out PATH deep links -- /node/aawsap,
# /path/aawsap/aaro, /band/custody, /era/nd, /q/via:bigelow -- and site/_redirects
# rewrote all five to index.html so the old client router could read the path.
# The app is hash-routed, so those paths are no longer routes at all and every
# link already handed out would 404 after the cutover of 3 September 2026. These
# rules send them into the app instead.
#
# Fragments in a DESTINATION are supported, and so is :splat alongside one:
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/redirects/ states that a
# destination "Can include fragments, query strings, splats, and placeholders",
# and gives `/page/ /page2/#fragment 301` as an example. Fragments in a SOURCE are
# not evaluated, which does not matter here: every source is a plain path.
#
# 302, not 301. These are a courtesy for links that predate the cutover, not a
# permanent scheme, and a 301 would be cached in readers' browsers indefinitely.

# Exact equivalents. A two-id /path/ link was a route between two nodes, and
# since redesign phase 10 the app has one: Connect. It must come before the
# one-id rule, because the first matching rule wins.
/node/*       /#/n/:splat            302
/path/:a/:b   /#/connect/:a/:b       302
/path/*       /#/t/:splat            302

# Approximate. The app's era columns are on the timeline, and ?era= is the
# section 2.1 parameter for them.
/era/*    /#/timeline?era=:splat 302

# No equivalent. The app has no per-band screen; the map is where bands are shown,
# so a band link lands on the map rather than nowhere.
/band/*   /#/map                 302

# No equivalent. The old /q/ links were saved searches in a query language the app
# does not have. Home is the honest destination.
/q/*      /                      302

# A NOTE ON /path/. The old links carried two node ids (/path/aawsap/aaro, a route
# between two nodes). Until phase 10 they fell to the one-id rule and reached the
# not-found page; they now open #/connect/<a>/<b>, the routes between the two.
# A one-id /path/ link still goes to the thread of that id, as before.
